Begin Exploring Related Columns
Relevant tables / columns and how they will be used  

artists  
• ArtistId : primary key, lets us connect an artist with the artist’s albums/tracks and, finally, the sales made for that artist.  
• Name : so we can recognise / display the artist (also useful to double‑check who the best‑selling artist is).  

albums  
• AlbumId : link between tracks and artists.  
• ArtistId : tells which artist owns every album (join condition to artists).  

tracks  
• TrackId : link between invoice_items and albums.  
• AlbumId : tells which album every track belongs to (join condition to albums).  

invoice_items  
• InvoiceId : link to the invoice that the purchased tracks belong to.  
• TrackId : link to the exact track that was bought.  
• UnitPrice & Quantity : multiply, then SUM → dollar value of every sold track.  

invoices  
• InvoiceId : primary key, connects invoice_items with the invoice header.  
• CustomerId : who paid the invoice (join condition to customers).  

customers  
• CustomerId : primary key, links to invoices.  
• FirstName : what the task finally has to return.  

Main conditions we will probably use  
• albums.ArtistId = artists.ArtistId  
• tracks.AlbumId  = albums.AlbumId  
• invoice_items.TrackId = tracks.TrackId  
• invoice_items.InvoiceId = invoices.InvoiceId  
• invoices.CustomerId = customers.CustomerId  
• HAVING SUM(invoice_items.UnitPrice*invoice_items.Quantity) < 1   (per customer)  
• artist chosen must be the best‑selling one:
  ArtistId IN (SELECT … ORDER BY SUM(sales) DESC LIMIT 1)

--------------------------------------------------------------------
10 exploratory SQL queries (each limited to 20 rows)  
--------------------------------------------------------------------

```sql
-- 1. List some artists (basic inspection)
SELECT DISTINCT ArtistId, Name
FROM artists
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Show a few albums with their artists
SELECT al.AlbumId, al.Title, ar.Name AS Artist
FROM albums AS al
JOIN artists AS ar ON al.ArtistId = ar.ArtistId
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Look at a handful of invoice items (unit price & quantity)
SELECT InvoiceLineId, InvoiceId, TrackId, UnitPrice, Quantity
FROM invoice_items
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Rough sales per artist (who sells the most?)
SELECT ar.ArtistId,
       ar.Name,
       ROUND(SUM(ii.UnitPrice * ii.Quantity),2) AS Sales
FROM artists  AS ar
JOIN albums   AS al ON ar.ArtistId = al.ArtistId
JOIN tracks   AS t  ON al.AlbumId  = t.AlbumId
JOIN invoice_items AS ii ON t.TrackId = ii.TrackId
GROUP BY ar.ArtistId
ORDER BY Sales DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Identify the single best‑selling artist (top 1)
SELECT ar.ArtistId,
       ar.Name,
       ROUND(SUM(ii.UnitPrice * ii.Quantity),2) AS Sales
FROM artists  AS ar
JOIN albums   AS al ON ar.ArtistId = al.ArtistId
JOIN tracks   AS t  ON al.AlbumId  = t.AlbumId
JOIN invoice_items AS ii ON t.TrackId = ii.TrackId
GROUP BY ar.ArtistId
ORDER BY Sales DESC
LIMIT 1;
```

```sql
-- 6. See how much each customer spent for THAT best‑selling artist
SELECT c.CustomerId,
       c.FirstName,
       ROUND(SUM(ii.UnitPrice * ii.Quantity),2) AS AmountSpent
FROM (SELECT ar2.ArtistId             -- sub‑query just returns the top artist id
      FROM artists   ar2
      JOIN albums    al2 ON ar2.ArtistId = al2.ArtistId
      JOIN tracks    t2  ON al2.AlbumId  = t2.AlbumId
      JOIN invoice_items ii2 ON t2.TrackId = ii2.TrackId
      GROUP BY ar2.ArtistId
      ORDER BY SUM(ii2.UnitPrice * ii2.Quantity) DESC
      LIMIT 1) AS topArtist
JOIN albums   al   ON al.ArtistId = topArtist.ArtistId
JOIN tracks   t    ON t.AlbumId   = al.AlbumId
JOIN invoice_items ii ON ii.TrackId = t.TrackId
JOIN invoices i     ON i.InvoiceId = ii.InvoiceId
JOIN customers c    ON c.CustomerId = i.CustomerId
GROUP BY c.CustomerId
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. The same as #6, but only customers who spent < $1
SELECT c.CustomerId,
       c.FirstName,
       ROUND(SUM(ii.UnitPrice * ii.Quantity),2) AS AmountSpent
FROM (SELECT ar2.ArtistId
      FROM artists ar2
      JOIN albums  al2 ON ar2.ArtistId = al2.ArtistId
      JOIN tracks  t2  ON al2.AlbumId  = t2.AlbumId
      JOIN invoice_items ii2 ON t2.TrackId = ii2.TrackId
      GROUP BY ar2.ArtistId
      ORDER BY SUM(ii2.UnitPrice * ii2.Quantity) DESC
      LIMIT 1) AS topArtist
JOIN albums   al   ON al.ArtistId = topArtist.ArtistId
JOIN tracks   t    ON t.AlbumId   = al.AlbumId
JOIN invoice_items ii ON ii.TrackId = t.TrackId
JOIN invoices i     ON i.InvoiceId = ii.InvoiceId
JOIN customers c    ON c.CustomerId = i.CustomerId
GROUP BY c.CustomerId
HAVING AmountSpent < 1
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8. Same idea, but list invoice lines to validate amounts (<$1)
SELECT ii.InvoiceId,
       c.FirstName,
       ar.Name  AS Artist,
       ii.UnitPrice * ii.Quantity AS LineAmount
FROM invoice_items ii
JOIN invoices i     ON i.InvoiceId  = ii.InvoiceId
JOIN customers c    ON c.CustomerId = i.CustomerId
JOIN tracks t       ON t.TrackId    = ii.TrackId
JOIN albums al      ON al.AlbumId   = t.AlbumId
JOIN artists ar     ON ar.ArtistId  = al.ArtistId
WHERE ar.ArtistId = (
      SELECT ar2.ArtistId
      FROM artists ar2
      JOIN albums  al2 ON ar2.ArtistId = al2.ArtistId
      JOIN tracks  t2  ON al2.AlbumId  = t2.AlbumId
      JOIN invoice_items ii2 ON t2.TrackId = ii2.TrackId
      GROUP BY ar2.ArtistId
      ORDER BY SUM(ii2.UnitPrice * ii2.Quantity) DESC
      LIMIT 1)
  AND (ii.UnitPrice * ii.Quantity) < 1
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9. Customers + amount, using DISTINCT with a CTE for readability
WITH best AS (
     SELECT ar.ArtistId
     FROM artists ar
     JOIN albums  al ON ar.ArtistId = al.ArtistId
     JOIN tracks  t  ON al.AlbumId  = t.AlbumId
     JOIN invoice_items ii ON t.TrackId = ii.TrackId
     GROUP BY ar.ArtistId
     ORDER BY SUM(ii.UnitPrice*ii.Quantity) DESC
     LIMIT 1
)
SELECT DISTINCT c.FirstName,
       ROUND(SUM(ii.UnitPrice * ii.Quantity),2) AS AmountSpent
FROM best
JOIN albums   al   ON al.ArtistId = best.ArtistId
JOIN tracks   t    ON t.AlbumId   = al.AlbumId
JOIN invoice_items ii ON ii.TrackId = t.TrackId
JOIN invoices i     ON i.InvoiceId = ii.InvoiceId
JOIN customers c    ON c.CustomerId = i.CustomerId
GROUP BY c.CustomerId
HAVING AmountSpent < 1
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10. FINAL ANSWER: first names + amount spent (< $1) on albums of the best‑selling artist
SELECT c.FirstName,
       ROUND(SUM(ii.UnitPrice * ii.Quantity),2) AS AmountSpent
FROM customers c
JOIN invoices      i  ON c.CustomerId = i.CustomerId
JOIN invoice_items ii ON i.InvoiceId  = ii.InvoiceId
JOIN tracks        t  ON ii.TrackId  = t.TrackId
JOIN albums        al ON t.AlbumId   = al.AlbumId
JOIN artists       ar ON al.ArtistId = ar.ArtistId
WHERE ar.ArtistId = (
      SELECT ar2.ArtistId
      FROM artists ar2
      JOIN albums  al2 ON ar2.ArtistId = al2.ArtistId
      JOIN tracks  t2  ON al2.AlbumId  = t2.AlbumId
      JOIN invoice_items ii2 ON t2.TrackId = ii2.TrackId
      GROUP BY ar2.ArtistId
      ORDER BY SUM(ii2.UnitPrice * ii2.Quantity) DESC
      LIMIT 1)
GROUP BY c.CustomerId
HAVING AmountSpent < 1
ORDER BY AmountSpent ASC
LIMIT 20;
```

Running query #10 will return exactly the required list of customer first names together with how much (below $1) they spent on albums from the best‑selling artist.
